# Secrets for the Sona relay. Copy to sona.env, fill in, chmod 600.
# Generate each 32-byte key with:  head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64 | tr -d '='
#
# KT_SIGNING_KEY signs the Key Transparency log. LOSING it = every client's pinned key
# breaks. LEAKING it = an attacker can sign forged tree heads. Back it up separately
# and offline; never store it next to the database volume.
KT_SIGNING_KEY=

# STORAGE_KEY encrypts queued message blobs at rest. It must live OFF the data disk —
# that separation is what makes a stolen database undecryptable. This env file (on the
# host, not in the relay-data volume) satisfies that; a secrets manager is better.
STORAGE_KEY=

# Pseudonymizes rate-limit bookkeeping. Any random string; rotating it is harmless.
RATE_SALT=

# OPTIONAL — enables GIF search in clients. The relay proxies BOTH the search and the
# GIF bytes to Giphy, so user IPs and queries never reach the provider; a chosen GIF is
# re-sent as an ordinary end-to-end-encrypted attachment. Leave empty to disable (the
# clients then hide the GIF button). Get a key at https://developers.giphy.com/.
GIPHY_API_KEY=

# OPTIONAL — relay discoverability tier (docs/DEPLOYMENT.md "Private relays"):
#   open    (default) anyone may use the relay.
#   token   every request must carry the shared access token (x-sona-access header);
#           without it: 401. The relay is visible but unusable to strangers.
#   stealth token required AND every rejected request is a bare 404 — a scanner cannot
#           tell the relay from an empty web server. Also disables the QUIC media port
#           (it would answer probes before any token check); calls use WebSocket.
#ACCESS_MODE=token

# Shared access token(s) for token/stealth mode, comma-separated (a list so you can
# rotate gracefully: add the new one, migrate members, drop the old). Min 16 chars.
# Give the token to members together with the relay URL; the client's connect screen
# has an "Access token" field under Advanced. ONE token shared by everyone is
# deliberate: per-user credentials would let the relay attribute sealed-sender sends.
# Generate:  head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64 | tr -d '='
#RELAY_ACCESS_TOKENS=

# OPTIONAL — IP allowlist, comma-separated addresses/CIDRs (e.g. 10.8.0.0/24,203.0.113.7).
# Off when empty. Checked against the proxy's X-Real-IP, fail-closed. Practical for
# VPN/static-IP setups; roaming phones change addresses too often for this.
#IP_ALLOWLIST=

# Max concurrent delivery WebSockets per client address (default 16).
#MAX_WS_PER_IP=16

# Global ceiling on stored attachment + history-sync bytes (default 10 GiB). Per-address
# byte budgets bound one client; this bounds the sum, so uploads spread across many
# addresses still can't fill the disk. Over the ceiling uploads get HTTP 507; expiry
# (BLOB_TTL_DAYS attachments, 7-day sync blobs) frees space continuously.
#MAX_STORAGE_BYTES=10737418240
# Hard attachment retention cap in days (default 30). Blobs are deleted when it
# elapses regardless of chat state — E2EE deletes + sealed-sender uploads mean the
# relay cannot tie a blob to a message, so the schedule is its only honest deletion.
#BLOB_TTL_DAYS=30

# OPTIONAL — single-use registration invite codes, comma-separated (min 8 chars each).
# Non-empty = creating a BRAND-NEW account requires an unused code; key rotations,
# renames, and linked devices are never gated, so existing users can't be locked out.
# Works with any ACCESS_MODE: the token controls who can reach the relay, codes control
# who can join it (each new claim grows the permanent transparency log). Consumed codes
# are remembered in the database; add fresh codes here and restart to invite more people.
# Generate a few:  for i in 1 2 3; do head -c 12 /dev/urandom | base64 | tr -d '='; done
#REGISTRATION_CODES=
